--TEST--
`Core\Sse::upgrade` accepts `rule:security/isolate-shares-nothing`'s second entry spelling — a static method reference — and it reaches the same body the path form does rather than any compile question — `rule:concurrency/an-upgrade-is-spawn-shaped` and `rule:concurrency/two-doors-one-isolate`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:concurrency/two-doors-one-isolate` gives this member "the identical clause" its sibling takes, so
// the operand is § 2's under ADR 0006 § *Decision*: a file path, or a static
// method whose parameters `args:` binds by name. This case is the accepted
// half of that rule at the second door;
// `an-upgrade-refuses-an-entry-it-cannot-see-the-function-behind.nvst` is the
// refused half, written once at the first.
class Feed {
    public static function run(string $room): void {
        echo "ran ", $room, "\n";
    }
}

// A script run from the command line is a request no server is answering, so
// what says the entry was *accepted* is that the member reaches its own body
// and reports the missing cell from there -- byte for byte the report the path
// form gets, never a diagnostic and never a silent `void`. The cell is asked
// about before the entry is read, which is why both spellings answer alike;
// `nvs_stdlib::socket`'s § *The method form waits on a name* owns what this
// form still cannot prepare once there is a cell to prepare it into, and it is
// one rule for both doors.
try {
    Core\Sse::upgrade(Feed::run(...), args: {room: "lobby"});
    echo "opened\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The entry is *named*, not called: `ran lobby` is nowhere above, because the
// isolate § 5 opens is the only place that body ever runs.
echo "not run here\n";
--EXPECT--
`Core\Sse::upgrade` needs a request a server is answering and this is not one: every request the server runs is offered an event stream, and a program run from the command line, a `spawn script` child and a `#[Test]` method are not requests
not run here
